CAT = "phcleLea"; SEG = "LEA"
# Law Enforcement Administration: organization and administration (R.A. 6975, R.A. 8551,
# R.A. 11200), comparative policing, industrial security, operations and planning, leadership.
Q = [
("The Philippine National Police was created by:",
 "R.A. 6975", ["R.A. 8551", "R.A. 11131", "P.D. 765"],
 "R.A. 6975, the Department of the Interior and Local Government Act of 1990, created the PNP. R.A. 8551 of 1998 reformed and reorganized it; P.D. 765 had formed the Integrated National Police."),
("The PNP Reform and Reorganization Act of 1998 is:",
 "R.A. 8551", ["R.A. 6975", "R.A. 9263", "R.A. 10973"],
 "R.A. 8551 amended R.A. 6975 to professionalize the PNP: it created the Internal Affairs Service, set qualification standards and introduced attrition. R.A. 9263 professionalized the BFP and BJMP."),
("The PNP is under the administrative control and operational supervision of:",
 "The National Police Commission", ["The Department of Justice", "The Armed Forces of the Philippines", "The Civil Service Commission"],
 "Under R.A. 6975 as amended, the PNP is placed under NAPOLCOM, which is attached to the DILG for policy and program coordination. Mayors and governors, as deputized representatives of the Commission, direct the employment and deployment of local police units."),
("The office created by R.A. 8551 to investigate police misconduct from within the PNP is the:",
 "Internal Affairs Service", ["People's Law Enforcement Board", "Commission on Human Rights", "Office of the Ombudsman"],
 "R.A. 8551 created the Internal Affairs Service to conduct investigations of PNP personnel motu proprio or on complaint. The People's Law Enforcement Board was created earlier by R.A. 6975 to hear citizen complaints."),
("Citizen complaints against police officers that may lead to dismissal are heard at the local level by the:",
 "People's Law Enforcement Board", ["Barangay Lupon", "Regional Trial Court", "Provincial Board"],
 "R.A. 6975 created the People's Law Enforcement Board in each city and municipality to hear citizen complaints against uniformed PNP members. The Lupon handles barangay conciliation, not police discipline."),
("Under R.A. 11200, the highest rank in the PNP is:",
 "Police General", ["Police Director General", "Police Colonel", "Police Lieutenant General"],
 "R.A. 11200 (2019) renamed the PNP ranks. The highest is Police General, held by the Chief PNP; it replaced Police Director General."),
("Under R.A. 11200, the lowest rank of a uniformed PNP member is:",
 "Patrolman or Patrolwoman", ["Police Corporal", "Police Officer 1", "Police Staff Sergeant"],
 "R.A. 11200 replaced Police Officer 1 with Patrolman or Patrolwoman as the entry rank. Police Corporal is the next rank up."),
("Under R.A. 8551, the compulsory retirement age of PNP uniformed personnel is:",
 "56", ["60", "65", "57"],
 "R.A. 8551 sets compulsory retirement for PNP officers and non-commissioned officers at 56 years of age. 65 is the general compulsory retirement age in government service."),
("The Bureau of Fire Protection and the Bureau of Jail Management and Penology are under the:",
 "Department of the Interior and Local Government", ["Department of Justice", "Department of National Defense", "Office of the President"],
 "R.A. 6975 created the BFP and the BJMP under the DILG, alongside the PNP. The Bureau of Corrections is under the Department of Justice."),
("Who is regarded as the father of modern policing for the Metropolitan Police Act of 1829?",
 "Sir Robert Peel", ["August Vollmer", "Henry Fielding", "O. W. Wilson"],
 "Peel's 1829 Act created the London Metropolitan Police, whose officers were nicknamed bobbies or peelers. August Vollmer is credited as the father of modern American police administration."),
("The International Criminal Police Organization (INTERPOL) has its General Secretariat in:",
 "Lyon, France", ["Vienna, Austria", "Geneva, Switzerland", "The Hague, Netherlands"],
 "INTERPOL's General Secretariat is in Lyon. It was founded in Vienna in 1923, and The Hague hosts Europol."),
("An INTERPOL notice seeking the location and arrest of a wanted person with a view to extradition is a:",
 "Red Notice", ["Blue Notice", "Yellow Notice", "Black Notice"],
 "A Red Notice asks police worldwide to locate and provisionally arrest a person pending extradition. Blue seeks information on a person, Yellow helps find missing persons and Black concerns unidentified bodies."),
("The small neighborhood police post in Japan, staffed by officers who know the area closely, is the:",
 "Koban", ["Chuzaisho", "Gendarmerie", "Precinct"],
 "The koban is the urban police box, a model of community policing studied in comparative policing. The chuzaisho is the residential police post in rural areas, where the officer lives with his family."),
("A policing system in which a single national government directs the police throughout the country is:",
 "Centralized", ["Fragmented", "Decentralized", "Integrated"],
 "A centralized system is directed by one national authority. A fragmented or decentralized system, like that of the United States, spreads police powers among many local and state agencies."),
("Under the principle of span of control, a supervisor should:",
 "Direct only as many people as he can manage well", ["Report to more than one superior", "Delegate all his authority", "Direct every member of the unit personally"],
 "Span of control limits the number of subordinates one supervisor can effectively direct. Reporting to only one superior is the separate principle of unity of command."),
("The formal line of authority through which orders pass from the top of the police organization down to the lowest rank is the:",
 "Chain of command", ["Span of control", "Line of duty", "Staff function"],
 "The chain of command is the hierarchy of authority. Staff functions advise and support the line, which carries out the main police work."),
("The acronym POSDCORB, describing the functions of an administrator, was coined by:",
 "Luther Gulick", ["Max Weber", "Frederick Taylor", "Henri Fayol"],
 "Luther Gulick summarized the work of the chief executive as Planning, Organizing, Staffing, Directing, Co-ordinating, Reporting and Budgeting. Henri Fayol had earlier named five functions of management."),
("A leader who makes decisions alone and expects subordinates to obey without discussion uses which style?",
 "Autocratic", ["Democratic", "Laissez-faire", "Participative"],
 "An autocratic leader centralizes decisions. A democratic or participative leader involves subordinates; a laissez-faire leader leaves them largely alone."),
("A plan that sets the long-range goals and direction of the whole police organization is:",
 "Strategic", ["Tactical", "Procedural", "Operational"],
 "Strategic plans cover the organization's long-range direction. Tactical plans address specific situations at a given place and time; procedural plans set standard steps for routine work."),
("Patrol is often called the backbone of police work because it:",
 "Creates police presence that deters crime", ["Replaces the work of criminal investigators", "Is done only at night", "Involves the fewest officers"],
 "Patrol puts officers where crimes can happen, giving the sense of police omnipresence that deters offenders, and it answers calls for service. It is usually the largest unit of a police force."),
("Which type of patrol gives the closest contact with the community but the smallest area covered?",
 "Foot patrol", ["Mobile patrol", "Air patrol", "Marine patrol"],
 "Foot patrol lets officers know residents and spot small problems, but it covers less ground. Mobile patrol covers wider areas and responds faster."),
("Mapping where crimes cluster so that patrols can be focused on those places identifies:",
 "Hot spots", ["Beats", "Sectors", "Choke points"],
 "Crime mapping shows hot spots, places where crime concentrates, so resources go where they prevent the most crime. Beats and sectors are patrol divisions, not crime patterns."),
("The three major aspects of industrial security are physical security, personnel security and:",
 "Document and information security", ["Executive protection and escort security", "Traffic security", "Cyber patrol"],
 "Industrial security is classically divided into physical, personnel, and document and information security. Fire safety and other concerns fall within physical security."),
("Under the classic perimeter standard, a clear zone between the perimeter barrier and exterior structures should be at least:",
 "20 feet", ["50 feet", "10 feet", "100 feet"],
 "Security texts call for a clear zone of 20 feet or more between the barrier and outside structures, and 50 feet or more between the barrier and structures inside the protected area, so intruders have no cover."),
("A critical examination of a facility to find its security weaknesses and recommend measures is a:",
 "Security survey", ["Security inspection", "Security clearance", "Security education"],
 "A security survey is the full, first assessment of a facility's protection. A security inspection checks later whether the recommended measures are being followed."),
("The doctrine that a person may access classified information only when his duties require it is:",
 "Need to know", ["Compartmentation", "Security clearance", "Classification"],
 "Need to know restricts access to those whose work requires the information, even if they hold a clearance. A clearance is the official finding that a person is eligible for a level of classified matter."),
("Under R.A. 11917, the law that governs private security agencies, security guards and the private security services industry is the:",
 "Private Security Services Industry Act", ["Private Security Agency Law of 1969", "Revised Fire Code", "Anti-Terrorism Act"],
 "R.A. 11917 (2022), the Private Security Services Industry Act, repealed R.A. 5487, the Private Security Agency Law of 1969, and sets the rules for agencies, guards and their training."),
("A security force maintained by a company for its own protection, rather than hired from an agency, is a:",
 "Company guard force", ["Private security agency", "Government guard unit", "Barangay peacekeeping action team"],
 "A company guard force is organized by an establishment for its own use. A private security agency contracts out its guards to clients."),
("The PNP program that brings police and residents together to identify and solve local crime problems is an example of:",
 "Community-oriented policing", ["Traditional reactive policing", "Military-style policing", "Selective enforcement"],
 "Community-oriented policing treats residents as partners in problem-solving and crime prevention. Reactive policing waits for calls for service."),
("An order specifying the mission, area and time of a police operation and the personnel and firearms involved is a:",
 "Mission order", ["Letter of instruction", "Memorandum receipt", "Mittimus"],
 "A mission order authorizes a specific operation and lists the personnel and equipment. A letter of instruction gives general guidance; a mittimus is a court order committing a person to jail."),
("The core of nationalism, as taught in character formation, is:",
 "Love of country and pride in being Filipino", ["Distrust of foreign nations and their people", "Loyalty to a political party", "Obedience to any superior order"],
 "Nationalism is love of country and a sense of national identity, expressed in service to the nation. It does not mean hostility to other nations or blind obedience."),
("In the PNP's rules on the use of force, the force used during an operation must be:",
 "Necessary and reasonable to the threat", ["The maximum available", "Decided by the suspect's record", "Approved in writing by a commander first"],
 "The PNP Police Operational Procedures limit force to what is necessary and reasonable to overcome resistance or a threat. Deadly force is a last resort when there is imminent danger of death or serious injury."),
]
